WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.425
[MUSIC - FELIX MENDELSSOHN, "WEDDING  MARCH"]

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.360
MARTIN PUCHNER: Thanks to Eckermann' notes,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.520
we know that the birth of world literature

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:24.060
took place in the afternoon of January 31, 1827 at Goethe's house.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.990
By the time, Eckermann had been in Weimar for over a year and a half

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.510
and had settled into a routine.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.570
A few times a week, he would have dinner at Goethe's.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.220
On that Wednesday, he hadn't been at Goethe's several days.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.510
So Goethe had all kinds of thoughts and reflections pent up

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:44.550
and was holding forth, reporting on what he had done and read

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.570
since they last met.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.910
Apparently he had been reading a Chinese novel.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:50.760
Really?

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.360
That must have been rather strange, Eckermann explains.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.560
But this was not the right reaction.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.250
After a year and a half, Eckermann had still not fully understood his master.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.010
No.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:07.790
Much less so than one thinks, Goethe reprimanded him and began to lecture.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.920
Eckermann liked it when Goethe lectured.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.330
There was always something to be learned.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.710
Goethe started about the influence of the British writer

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.060
Samuel Richardson on his own work, which Eckermann listened to

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.980
with polite interest.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.540
But before long, Goethe came back to Chinese novels en masse.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.790
Eckermann was still skeptical.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:27.500
What?

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.030
These Chinese novels are decent?

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.530
More decent than the French, Goethe replied.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.614
Which wasn't saying much.

00:01:34.614 --> 00:01:36.630
Eckermann ventured that this Chinese novel

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.480
must be quite an unusual, the exception to the rule.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.430
Wrong again.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.600
The master's voice was stern.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.040
Nothing could be further from the truth.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.840
The Chinese have thousands of them.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.480
And they had them when our ancestors were still living in the trees.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.670
Eckermann was speechless.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.300
Goethe took advantage of the silence and topped the claim

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.920
with a truly ludicrous one.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.920
The time has come for the epoch of world of literature.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.270
And everyone must seek to accelerate it.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:12.075
Everyone, and that includes you, you my young and ignorant friend,

00:02:12.075 --> 00:02:13.660
Goethe means.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.970
Everyone, and especially Eckermann, must accelerate the process of world

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.290
literature, must read Chinese novels.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.485
Thousands of them.

00:02:23.485 --> 00:02:27.970
Eckermann was shocked realizing that Goethe had opened a can of worms.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.670
But fortunately he got distracted.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.950
There was a noise outside the window.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.660
Excited, he jumped up and went to the window to look outside.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.340
And Goethe did the same.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.420
They were expecting the return of horse-drawn sledges

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.420
that had left in the morning, one of the more exciting things

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.740
that could happen on a Weimar late afternoon.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.910
Suddenly Eckermann and Goethe didn't care at all

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.370
about Chinese novels and world literature.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.510
They pressed their noses against the window

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.930
to get a better look at the street.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.500
But it wasn't the sledges.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.710
And so they got back to their seats disappointed.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:04.065
Where were we?

00:03:04.065 --> 00:03:04.565
Ah, yes.

00:03:04.565 --> 00:03:06.362
World Literature.

00:03:06.362 --> 00:03:10.160
Goethe started talking again until there was another noise outside.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.870
And Eckermann and Goethe once again dropped the conversation

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.700
to look out the window.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.850
But it still wasn't the sledges.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.830
And so they went back to their seats.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.440
And Goethe talked of this and that rather listlessly

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.500
until it was time for Eckermann to leave.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.530
This was the birth of world literature.

